diff --git a/docs/ARCHITECTURE.md b/docs/ARCHITECTURE.md
index e1b51628..4df2eb7e 100644
--- a/docs/ARCHITECTURE.md
+++ b/docs/ARCHITECTURE.md
@@ -314,6 +314,23 @@ new ones as they arrive. Without permission stand-ins are the app's colour and i
 is the wallpaper's file. Arbor never prompts for the permission from here (`WindowPictures.isAllowed` is
 for onboarding to ask), and nothing is written to disk.
 
+**The glider** (`Controller+Glider.swift`, `Glide` in ArborCore). With motion on, `windowPictures` on and a picture to be had at the act, one window travels
+as a picture of itself and there is no stage: `relayout` calls `takeOff` where it would call `stageTransition`, and the two never play together. The window is the one the
+act names (a command's target, the window that was dropped), or else the one with the keyboard; it has to be in view before and after, as itself, in nobody's hand, and
+go further than a trifle. `OverlayPresenter.liftOff` is synchronous and bounded: true only when the picture is up over the window, false when there is none in time, and then
+everything is as it is without a glider. So the controller stays deterministic, and a glide is never late: no picture, no glide. The other windows jump, in plain view.
+The real window's road is its old place, out of view, its new frame. It waits beside the display at the height of the frame it is going to, a point of it showing,
+where the size fits as it will at the end; each of the three steps is one `setFrame`. It is asked for its size there as the others are asked where they stand (`aimTheGlide`, called
+by `askingFirst` and `apply` with every layout): if it will not be as small as its slot, or a neighbour's answer changes the layout, it is asked again for the layout that follows, still out of
+view, and the panel is told where to go instead. Once it has an end, `windows` has it at that frame, so no other part of Arbor
+knows that it is away, and what its app reports of its place meanwhile is dropped (`whatIsNews`). What pays for taking the window in use out of view: the arrangement is
+saved and flushed before it leaves, and where it waits is a place every start fetches windows from; the timer `glide` brings it back after the glide's length, half a second at the
+most, whatever the presenter does; every command, press, drag, key of Arbor's, closing, and what only the presenter hears of (`glideWasInterrupted`: sleep, a
+Space) ends it at once at its end (`landTheGlider`: the window is placed, then the panel goes); a change of displays ends it at once where the window belongs on the displays as
+they are now, fetched from where it waits like any window they left stranded, because its end may be nowhere by then; and whatever else is not as foreseen (an answer that may be the screen's, an app that does not answer, a window that
+becomes a tile for its answer) calls it off: the books say where the window is, the layout under way places it, the panel
+goes. **On a Mac there is no panel yet**: `AppKitOverlays.liftOff` says no. `GliderTests`; the one rule by which its road is judged is `Glide.whatWasSeen`, which every detector reads the log through (`FakeWindowSystem.asSeen`; `glider_came_down` in `jolts.py`).
+
 ## What a command costs
 
 Measured on a Mac with twelve real windows: Arbor's own
